Apple enlisted British musician Labrinth to create a new song during an overnight livestream, demonstrating the new touchscreen <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/macbook-pro/">MacBook Pro</a> and Logic Pro at Apple Tower Theatre in downtown Los Angeles.<br/>
<br/>
<div class="center-wrap"><iframe width="560" height="315" src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/UUvGJ5WM-gA" title="Labrinth Live on Mac" frameborder="0" allowfullscreen></iframe></div><br/>
The session, titled "Labrinth Live on Mac," is now available to watch as a replay lasting almost four hours. It started on November 4 at 8 p.m. Pacific, or 11 p.m. Eastern, with Labrinth tasked with creating a song from scratch. He can be seen reaching onto the &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj;'s display to manipulate Logic Pro's mixer controls, with a multitrack arrangement visible onscreen. Viewers were invited to submit questions as he worked, and the finished song is due for release on <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/apple-music/">Apple Music</a> next week. <br/>
<br/>
Apple initially teased the collaboration on TikTok with a white box bearing Labrinth's name and instructions to leave it unopened until November 4. <br/>
<br/>
Music production was among the uses Apple demonstrated when it <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/10/28/apple-new-macbook-pro/">unveiled the new MacBook Pro</a> last week. Logic Pro includes an onscreen keyboard, chord pads, and drum pads that musicians can play by touch, including playing multiple notes at once.<br/>
<br/>
Labrinth is a singer, songwriter, and producer known for solo releases including "Jealous" and "Earthquake," as well as his music for HBO's "Euphoria." He also forms the group LSD with Sia and Diplo.<br/>
<br/>
